  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nStorms are beginning in western/central Ohio associated with\napproaching low pressure. CAMs have latched onto the storm\ndevelopment, but continue to struggle with the system's\nmovement, with 18Z runs suggesting more of an ewd trajectory\nafter 12Z runs' sewd movement.\n\nRegardless of the system movement, the environment remains\nprimed for heavy rainfall, with 00Z RAOB indicating PWAT value \njust under 2.00\" and modest low-level buoyancy almost wholly \nbeneath the freezing level. Any initial low-topped showers will \nheavily feature warm-rain processes, efficient rainfall and very\nlittle lightning.\n\nOne or more localized axes of 1.5-2.5\" rain seems possible \novernight somewhere from PHD/ZZV-HLG-MGW, but where these axes\nof heaviest rainfall eventually materialize still remains\nuncertain. Wherever they occur, however, flash flooding will be\npossible given prior wet ground, and heightened awareness is\nwarranted given the unusual time of day for flash flooding.\n\nWith continued deep moisture advection into the region, PWAT \ncould climb as high as 2.20-2.25\". If the 12Z sounding records \nPWAT values in that range, it would be one of the highest such \nreadings ever recorded at KPIT (since 1952). Regardless, PWAT\nvalues are anomalously high, and a prolonged period of potential\nheavy rainfall from multiple rounds of storms remains in the\noffing. The Flood Watch will continue until Monday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nConfidence in what follows for Sun and Sun night drops off, as \natmospheric recovery/Sun-afternoon storm evolution will depend \non the mesoscale setup left behind by the overnight complex. \nWhile stable morning air should limit coverage and intensity of \nconvection at least into early afternoon, redevelopment appears \nincreasingly likely tied to a potential mesoscale convective \nvortex developing from storms in the Midwest/northern Plains.\n\nHere, a pocket of 25-30 knots of 0-6km shear could provide \nenough storm organization for a damaging wind potential, while \nelongated/curved low-level hodographs (particularly enhanced by\na MCV if present) and sufficient 0-1km SRH suggest a lower-end \ntornado threat for esewd-moving storms. Machine-learning \nguidance does support these assertions, likely picking up on\nMCV-enhanced fields. Also, the same favorable heavy-rainfall \ningredients from the previous night will continue, extending the\nflash flood threat into Sun night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nDiurnal stabilization should begin to limit convective coverage\nand intensity late Sun evening, with a few showers lingering \ninto Mon with one last shortwave trough. By this point, PWAT \nwill be dropping off thanks to developing drier nwly flow, \nwhich should help to taper the heavy-rainfall threat. High \npressure building in from the Great Lakes should end \nprecipitation by Mon evening. Dry weather is then favored until\nWed, when the next front could potentially arrive, although \nthis timing could change.",
